<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haakon VII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Prince Carl of Denmark, Haakon VII of Norway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haakon VII was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1905 to 1957, born Prince Carl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. After Norway dissolved its union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1905, a referendum confirmed him as king, and he took a traditional Norwegian royal name. During the German invasion of 1940 he refused to accept a collaborationist government and led the government in exile from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, becoming a symbol of national resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haakon-vii.jpg" alt="Haakon VII" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Library of Norway · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrett_av_Kong_Haakon_VII_-_King_Haakon_VII,_1946_(6817780248)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
